  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "The offense couldn't overcome early damage when it mattered most.",
    "what_this_means": "Los Angeles built a 6-1 lead by the fourth inning and Athletics rallied just enough to make it interesting. Joshua Kuroda-Grauer went 3-for-4 and the lineup scraped together 11 hits. But three of their 13 runners in scoring position converted into runs. The real break came in the third, when with runners loaded they managed nothing, runners stayed on base, and the deficit kept growing. Gage Jump exited after 4.2 innings having given up five earned runs on 11 hits, and the bullpen that followed. Matt Krook immediately surrendering three more in just 0.1 innings. Couldn't recover the damage done. This is how deficits become final: early miscues stack faster than late heroics can erase them, and one clean inning becoming two becomes the difference between trailing by one and chasing all night. They had the hits. They didn't have the timing.",
    "what_to_watch": "Wrobleski brings a 2.71 ERA into tonight's rematch at 6:40 PT, and Jeffrey Springs takes the mound for Oakland needing to keep the Dodgers off the board early.",
